        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "HhtdS",
          "name": "Note · B6 + B11",
          "width": 1600,
          "fill": "#FFFFFF",
          "cornerRadius": 12,
          "stroke": "#2357C9",
          "strokeWidth": {
            "left": 3
          },
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "padding": [
            16,
            20
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "wymUW",
              "name": "Note · B6 + B11",
              "fill": "#2357C9",
              "content": "Note · B6 + B11",
              "fontFamily": "$font-text",
              "fontSize": 13,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "vh78y",
              "name": "Chose: keep the B6 monitor a",
              "fill": "#262B3A",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Chose: draw the decided B6 monitor look as a proposal (today's watch uses Halo's card styles: LIVE badge, sources, notes), and give it life that is information, not decoration: a 2 px progress line, a step counter with an estimate, steps that tick from › to ✓ with their times, sources with a state each, a LIVE dot that breathes. Rejected: a spinner (that's the Working card's mark, and B3 needs them apart); a thumbnail of the app (privacy, and stale the moment it's drawn); a percentage (worker steps vary too much). Question for the owner: should the estimate (\"about 1 min left\") be shown at all before Swift has seen two steps finish?",
              "lineHeight": 1.5,
              "fontFamily": "$font-text",
              "fontSize": 15,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "THxdX",
          "name": "Note · B9",
          "width": 1600,
          "fill": "#FFFFFF",
          "cornerRadius": 12,
          "stroke": "#2357C9",
          "strokeWidth": {
            "left": 3
          },
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "padding": [
            16,
            20
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "O59K3g",
              "name": "Note · B9",
              "fill": "#2357C9",
              "content": "Note · B9",
              "fontFamily": "$font-text",
              "fontSize": 13,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "MSxxR",
              "name": "Chose: three placements behi",
              "fill": "#262B3A",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Chose: three placements behind one Dock button. Beside the card is today's. The docked sidebar is an app bar on the orb's edge, so Windows shrinks the work area instead of the watch covering anything, and the orb moves to its inner edge by the A10 rule. The mini sits in the orb-side corner opposite the orb. Rejected: a floating, draggable watch (one more thing to manage, and it ends up over your work); docking on the far edge (it splits one run across the screen). Question for the owner: should Swift remember the placement per kind of run (research docks, quick lookups stay beside), or just reuse the last one?",
              "lineHeight": 1.5,
              "fontFamily": "$font-text",
              "fontSize": 15,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "Q5Enf",
          "name": "Note · B10",
          "width": 1600,
          "fill": "#FFFFFF",
          "cornerRadius": 12,
          "stroke": "#2357C9",
          "strokeWidth": {
            "left": 3
          },
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 6,
          "padding": [
            16,
            20
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "v7VC8",
              "name": "Note · B10",
              "fill": "#2357C9",
              "content": "Note · B10",
              "fontFamily": "$font-text",
              "fontSize": 13,
              "fontWeight": "700"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "hF1Kr",
              "name": "Chose: one run, two sizes, t",
              "fill": "#262B3A",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Chose: one run, two sizes, three ties. The same title, the same clock to the second, and an accent tether from the watch's title bar to the card or edge, plus a Run link chip on the card. While the watch is open the card never fades; when the run ends both say so at once, and the card's reading time starts only after you close the watch. Rejected: hiding the card while the watch is open (that broke the deck's count and the orb badge); merging them into one big surface (it loses the deck, and the watch would have to become a card). Question for the owner: when the watch is docked, is the Run link chip enough, or should the tether reach the sidebar too?",
              "lineHeight": 1.5,
              "fontFamily": "$font-text",
              "fontSize": 15,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "g6GdeQ",
          "name": "Note · B12",
